The statement is **true** (for $p \geq 1$, which is the intended reading of the notation $\mathbb{R}^{n+p}\to\mathbb{R}^n$).

## Proof

Assume, for contradiction, that there exists a continuous injective map
$$f:\mathbb{R}^{n+p}\to\mathbb{R}^n,\qquad p\geq 1.$$

**Step 1: Slicing the domain.** For each $v\in\mathbb{R}^p$, define the affine slice
$$L_v \;=\; \mathbb{R}^n\times\{v\}\;\subseteq\;\mathbb{R}^{n+p},$$
which is canonically homeomorphic to $\mathbb{R}^n$. The slices $\{L_v\}_{v\in\mathbb{R}^p}$ are pairwise disjoint and partition $\mathbb{R}^{n+p}$.

Let $g_v := f\big|_{L_v}:L_v\to\mathbb{R}^n$. Each $g_v$ is continuous and (since $f$ is injective) injective. Identifying $L_v\cong\mathbb{R}^n$, we have a continuous injection $g_v:\mathbb{R}^n\to\mathbb{R}^n$.

**Step 2: Applying invariance of domain.** By the **invariance of domain** theorem (Brouwer, 1912): *if $U\subseteq\mathbb{R}^n$ is open and $\varphi:U\to\mathbb{R}^n$ is continuous and injective, then $\varphi(U)$ is open in $\mathbb{R}^n$.*

Taking $U=\mathbb{R}^n$ (which is open in itself) and $\varphi=g_v$, we conclude that
$$U_v \;:=\; g_v(L_v) \;=\; f(L_v)$$
is an **open** subset of $\mathbb{R}^n$ for every $v\in\mathbb{R}^p$.

**Step 3: Disjointness of the images.** Since the slices $L_v$ are pairwise disjoint and $f$ is injective, the images $U_v=f(L_v)$ are **pairwise disjoint**:
$$v\neq v' \;\Longrightarrow\; U_v\cap U_{v'}=\varnothing.$$

Each $U_v$ is nonempty (it is the image of the nonempty set $L_v$), hence each $U_v$ is a **nonempty open** subset of $\mathbb{R}^n$.

**Step 4: Countability obstruction.** The family $\{U_v\}_{v\in\mathbb{R}^p}$ is a collection of pairwise disjoint nonempty open subsets of $\mathbb{R}^n$.

But $\mathbb{R}^n$ is **separable**: it has a countable dense subset, namely $\mathbb{Q}^n$. In any separable topological space, every collection of pairwise disjoint nonempty open sets is at most countable — indeed, each such open set contains at least one point of the countable dense set, and disjointness forces these points to be distinct, giving an injection from the collection into $\mathbb{Q}^n$.

Therefore $\{U_v\}_{v\in\mathbb{R}^p}$ is at most **countable**.

**Step 5: Contradiction.** Since $p\geq 1$, the index set $\mathbb{R}^p$ is **uncountable** (in fact of cardinality $2^{\aleph_0}$). The map $v\mapsto U_v$ is injective (disjointness of the $U_v$ means distinct $v$ give distinct $U_v$), so $\{U_v\}_{v\in\mathbb{R}^p}$ has the same cardinality as $\mathbb{R}^p$, hence is **uncountable**.

This contradicts Step 4, which says the family is at most countable.

The contradiction shows that no continuous injective map $f:\mathbb{R}^{n+p}\to\mathbb{R}^n$ can exist when $p\geq 1$. Equivalently, every continuous $f:\mathbb{R}^{n+p}\to\mathbb{R}^n$ fails to be injective. $\blacksquare$

**Remark.** The argument uses two ingredients: (i) invariance of domain, which forces each slice's image to be open; and (ii) separability of $\mathbb{R}^n$, which forbids uncountably many pairwise disjoint nonempty open sets. The case $p=0$ is excluded because then $\mathbb{R}^p$ is a single point and the countability obstruction vanishes — consistent with the fact that the identity $\mathbb{R}^n\to\mathbb{R}^n$ is a continuous injection.

### PROOF COMPLETE
